## Why the API alone isn't enough
An API response is the application's claim about what happened. A 201 Created with a JSON body is a promise, not proof. The database is what actually persisted, and comparing the two closes the gap between "the API said it worked" and "it worked." This is the same idea as UI-versus-DB checks from earlier Parts, moved one layer down, and it's usually cheaper: no browser, no rendering, just a request and a query.
The basic loop
```ts
test('creating a leave request persists exactly what the API returned', async ({ request }) => {
  const res = await request.post('/api/leave-requests', {
    data: { employeeId: 2, leaveTypeId: 1, startDate: '2027-03-01', endDate: '2027-03-02' },
  });
  expect(res.status()).toBe(201);
  const body = await res.json();                                   // { id: 512, status: 'pending', days: 2, ... }
```

```ts
  const [rows]: any = await db.execute(
    'SELECT leave_request_id, status, days, employee_id FROM leave_requests WHERE leave_request_id = ?', [body.id]);
| expect(rows).toHaveLength(1); | expect(rows[0].status).toBe(body.status); |
|---|---|
| expect(Number(rows[0].days)).toBe(body.days); | expect(rows[0].employee_id).toBe(2); |
```

  // and the side effect the response doesn't mention
```ts
  const [hist]: any = await db.execute(
    "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM leave_status_history WHERE leave_request_id = ? AND new_status = 'pending'", [body.id]);
  expect(hist[0].n).toBe(1);
```

  await db.execute('DELETE FROM leave_status_history WHERE leave_request_id = ?', [body.id]);   // cleanup, children first
  await db.execute('DELETE FROM leave_requests WHERE leave_request_id = ?', [body.id]);
});

Three levels of assertion are worth separating. Response contract: status code, shape, required fields. Persisted state: does the row exist with the right values (not just a row, but exactly what was requested, and nothing else changed). Side effects the response never mentions: history rows, balances, notifications, files. Testers who stop at the response miss the third level entirely, and that's where the interesting bugs from Parts 6 and 10 live (a status flips with no history row, a balance doesn't move).

| GET endpoints: the response must equal a query, not just "look plausible" | test('GET /api/employees/2 matches the row', async ({ request }) => { |
|---|---|
| const res = await request.get('/api/employees/2'); | const body = await res.json(); |

```ts
  const [rows]: any = await db.execute(
    `SELECT e.employee_id, e.first_name, e.last_name, e.email, d.name AS department, e.status
     FROM employees e LEFT JOIN departments d ON d.department_id = e.department_id
     WHERE e.employee_id = 2`);
  const r = rows[0];
```

  expect(body).toEqual({
| id: r.employee_id, firstName: r.first_name, lastName: r.last_name, | email: r.email, department: r.department, status: r.status, |
|---|---|
| }); | }); |

Building the expected object from the row (rather than hard-coding "Ravi", "Shrestha", ...) means the test survives data changes and works on any environment, which matters when the same suite runs against QA, staging and a fresh CI database with different seed data.
## List endpoints: compare the set of IDs, not just the count
A count match can hide wrong rows (two swapped, one missing and one extra cancel out). Compare identity:
```ts
test('GET /api/leave-requests?status=pending returns exactly the pending ones', async ({ request }) => {
  const res = await request.get('/api/leave-requests?status=pending');
  const body = await res.json();
  const apiIds = body.items.map((x: any) => x.id).sort((a: number, b: number) => a - b);
```

```ts
  const [rows]: any = await db.execute("SELECT leave_request_id FROM leave_requests WHERE status = 'pending' ORDER BY leave_request_id");
  const dbIds = rows.map((r: any) => r.leave_request_id);
```

  expect(apiIds).toEqual(dbIds);
});

Apply the same business filters the app applies (soft-delete, tenant, status) in your query, or you'll compare against the wrong population (2.1). If the API paginates, either fetch every page or compare against a matching LIMIT/OFFSET query (2.2), and always add a stable tiebreaker to ORDER BY on both sides.
## Error responses: the database must be untouched
The most commonly skipped assertion. When an API call should fail (validation error, conflict, not found, forbidden), always add a query proving nothing changed:
```ts
test('overlapping leave request is rejected and creates nothing', async ({ request }) => {
  const [before]: any = await db.execute('SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM leave_requests');
```

```ts
  const res = await request.post('/api/leave-requests', {
    data: { employeeId: 1, leaveTypeId: 1, startDate: '2026-10-13', endDate: '2026-10-13' },   // overlaps request 101
  });
  expect(res.status()).toBe(409);
```

```ts
  const [after]: any = await db.execute('SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM leave_requests');
  expect(after[0].n).toBe(before[0].n);                             // no phantom row left behind
});
```

Without this, a bug where the app returns an error message but still inserts the row (a rollback that silently failed, 7.3) passes every response-shape test and only shows up as a slowly growing table.
## Computed and derived fields: recompute independently
When a response includes a value the server calculated (a total, a balance, a formatted date), don't trust the server's own arithmetic as the oracle (5.3's "independent oracle" rule applies here too). Compute it from the raw rows:
```ts
test('leave summary total_approved_days is computed correctly', async ({ request }) => {
  const res = await request.get('/api/employees/2/leave-summary');
  const body = await res.json();
```

```ts
  const [rows]: any = await db.execute(
    "SELECT COALESCE(SUM(days),0) AS total FROM leave_requests WHERE employee_id = 2 AND status = 'approved'");
  expect(Number(body.totalApprovedDays)).toBe(Number(rows[0].total));
});
```

## Field-by-field, not object equality, when the API returns more than the DB
APIs often enrich a row with joined or formatted data (a department name instead of an ID, a human-readable date). toEqual on the whole object then fails for the wrong reason. Either build the expected object with the same enrichment (as in the GET example above) or assert field by field, and be explicit about formatting differences (money as a formatted string versus a raw decimal, dates as ISO versus a locale string).
## Idempotency and duplicate-prevention endpoints
Combine this chapter with 7.7's concurrency techniques: send the same creation request twice (with or without an idempotency key) and assert the database has one row, not two, using the anti-duplicate queries from 6.3.
